[{:seon.task/name "a-shard-says-what-each-test-waits-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Curated 2026-11-15 by context (root's ruling: a task's work is never a bin/ shell script; bin/ is a thin wrapper over an op, AGENTS §0): bin/test's shard waits (index order) are superseded by THE PLAN item 13 (bin/test a thin client of the test service, whose items' ends are messages): this task is what remains, the tests' own waits that have no end (below), fixed in the test code. Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; the owner: platform stability first). test side of the sweep (target/waits-test.md in the stability worktree): bin/test waits on its shards in index order (bin/test's wait $P[i]), so a hung shard 1 hides later shards' output; and test waits with no end beyond their answer: ts/ask's socket read of a live parked root, start-root!'s wait for ROOT LISTENING (167), stop-root! (207), kill-root! (178), binary-sh of a mode that parks (113), agent_test's and e2e's <!! event loops on an events tap (agent_test.clj; e2e.clj) that skip a dead agent's event, check.clj's await-event with no terminal event named (204,496,510,517,745,980,1156), bare @p / <!! after a synchronous step in procs_test (282-372), problems_test:176, step_test:11-20 (use the answer helper, procs_test.clj, or realized?). Correct: bin/test reports each shard as it ends (wait -n), and each test wait names its end: an agent's or root's death event ends an event loop (a loop that skips a death is red), a synchronous step's answer is read with realized? (no wait). The parked-everywhere case is jolt.async/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits. Clocks in test (check.clj; lib_test.clj) are listed in target/waits-test.md for docs/clocks.md's owner to name or remove.\n\nReopened 2026-11-15 by context (root's ruling): round 2's agent i71b01341317f9de merged this task's proof test and five scratch defs (seon.test-store/ck2, drive, lib, proto, proto2) but not its fix: bin/shards and the bin/test change were written to disk in the main tree, never to the store, and the proof's green verdict was judged on that disk copy (result/b059a5f7, bd923af9). Main's 12 merged keys are retracted. Still to do: the shard-ends reporting itself, through the program (bin/ as a thin wrapper over an op), with its proof green on files the store holds.\n\nCurated by its refine (agent c723bb0fc6b1b76f), from the run folded: what blocked it was its own one proof: the test it merged went red only at turn 29 of 30, and even then it read bin/test and bin/shards off the main tree's disk, so the run ended merged with the fix in no store but the tree's, and that proof and the scratch defs were retracted from main. Write the proof again, as ``seon.test-store-test/bin-test-reports-shard-ends-index-order-test``, green on files the store holds. The change site, read before anything else: `test-runner/prove!` (the planning part bin/test calls: it deals the shards) and `test-runner/shard!` (one shard's run, which knows when it ended) and `test-runner/-main` (the op bin/test and bin/prove dispatch to): the shard-ends reporting belongs in them, bin/ a thin wrapper, and its proof must read what the store holds, never the worktree's disk (a test that reads the checkout passes on what was never committed). The test waits the first paragraph lists (ask, start-root!, stop-root!, kill-root!, binary-sh, await-event) stay open for a task of their own: do not claim them done."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/bin-test-reports-shard-ends-index-order-test}}
 {:seon.task/name "three-tests-seed-more-than-their-claim-needs"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-07, by which verdicts hold seon.db/seed!: 33 tests seed on every run, 1.35 G ticks of a 28.6 G pass (4.7 percent); the rest of the seeds are a shard's first test taking the process's one in-memory store, which a clone cannot help. Most of the 33 test store creation itself, so no shared seeded store is worth building. Three are worth shrinking to their claims, about 2 percent: seon.proofs-test/a-process-plans-on-more-scratch-stores-than-its-undo-entries (235 M ticks, 12 seeds), seon.check-test/a-check-leaves-its-store-as-it-found-it (205 M, up from 36 M on 2026-11-06) and seon.prove-test/a-commit-starts-no-run-of-main-unless-the-config-says-so (141 M). Proof: each still red on the mutation it guards, at a smaller seed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/proofs-test-process-plans-more-scratch-stores-undo}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-system-test-ends-at-its-roots-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Tests. Found 2026-11-12 10:13 (jolt/tests' prove): with seon.reconcile/done? deleted, the test roots' server answered every request that summarizes tasks (seon.server/live-tasks -> task-summaries -> r/done?) with :seon.server/defect \"No such var: r/done?\" AT ONCE (bin/ask got it in under a second), yet about 30 seon.agent-test system tests each ran to their 300 s bound, parked on their socket to the root (bin/stalls' sample: kevent; lsof: one TCP connection to the root). So the root answered, and something on the test's side, or inside the tested agent's work (its turn reads live tasks too), treated the error as not yet and waited again. What allowed it: a wait whose only end is the bound when the thing it waits on answers an error (AGENTS.md section 4: every wait ends, with its value or a typed error). Plan: reproduce at n=1 (one with-agent test on a root whose server throws on task summaries), read which wait parked (a fiber dump of the test process and of the root at second 10), and make that wait end on the error it was given, at its cause; sweep test/seon for the same shape (a loop over root answers or events that recurs on anything but the one success). Proof, red first: that test ends within seconds with the root's error named, where today it runs to its bound."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/a-system-test-ends-at-its-roots-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-a-process-makes-is-its-agents-and-goes-at-its-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Needs runtime's fork-one (schedule item: a test run is an agent forked from its parent agent). Today `seon.test-store/reclaim!` sweeps after the fact: what a test process makes outside itself (a shared root's merges branch, its store main and runs dir, a fixture worktree) is named with its pid (mark-of), and the next shard removes what a gone pid made (kill -0); root, 2026-11-12: a stopgap. Plan: once fork-one lands, what a process makes is a fact on its agent entity (each branch, worktree and run dir it made, written as it makes it), and the system removes them at that agent's exit event, the same event that ends its run. Deletes: `seon.test-store/mark-of`, `seon.test-store/reclaim!`, dead-pid?, start-shared-root!'s shutdown hook, the fixtures' finally removals. Proof, red first: an agent that made a branch and a worktree and was killed leaves neither once its exit is recorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/what-an-agent-made-goes-at-its-exit}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-root-ends-with-the-run-that-started-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests; the structural fix is runtime's fork-one (family C of design/one-unit-spec.md: a test root becomes a child agent, stopped with its parent). Found 2026-11-12 (data): stopping a prove at 14:37 left a test's detached root (bin/held --detach, ./seon root --profile scripted) orphaned for 52 min, and bin/land later named its pids as processes working in data's worktree. A detached root is double-forked out of its starter's session on purpose, so its starter's end does not reach it. Plan: once fork-one lands, a system-test's root is the run's child agent and ends at the run's stop; until then, the test that starts a root records it on its run (mark-of's name, as reclaim! reads), and reclaim! ends a root whose maker is gone. Proof, red first: a run stopped while its test's root runs leaves no process of that root once the next shard starts."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/a-test-root-ends-with-the-run-that-started-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "fifos-live-outside-the-checkout", :seon.task/plan "Found by context, 2026-11-13 (notes: evidence/context-refine-hang-2026-11-13.md): the checkout holds FIFOs, runs/root.done (bin/system) and target/tmp/test-starter-*.fifo (seon.test-store), 4 of them stale from finished runs, and any agent's grep -r of its checkout can open one and block (a refine's grep sat in open(2) for 80 minutes). FIFOs move out of the tree (a temp dir of their run's), and a stale one ends with its run. Owner: tests. Proof: after a test run, no FIFO is left under the checkout.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/no-fifo-is-left-in-the-checkout}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-test-forks-the-running-runtime"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's order (2026-11-14, via root; his 11-11 ruling: an end-to-end test runs in a fork of the running runtime, never by booting from outside): right after proving runs as Flow (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer), each process test that boots its own root (seon.test-store/start-root!, binary-sh, in-agent's harness root) is rewritten to fork from the running runtime on its branch's env, worst offenders first; Stage 1 does not wait for all of them. Measured 2026-11-14 (grep of :seon.test/root, start-root!, binary-sh, in-agent): 223 tests, by namespace seon.agent-test 125, seon.repl-test 39, seon.server-test 27, seon.program-test 5, seon.system-test 5, seon.check-test 4, seon.test-store-test 4, seon.source-test 4, seon.task-files-test 2, seon.judge-test 2, and one each in seon.problems-test, seon.lib-test, seon.cost-test, seon.config-test, seon.reconcile-test, seon.turn-test. Their newest greens sum to 1,541 s. Worst first (newest green, ms): seon.agent-test/an-agent-ends-when-its-root-is-gone 145270, seon.server-test/a-named-prove-counts-for-status-and-the-judge-and-answers-one-line 75895, seon.turn-test/an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps 59761, seon.system-test/an-update-keeps-running-agents 58589, seon.judge-test/a-pass-stops-at-its-bound 45556, seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval 38386, seon.agent-test/a-hand-back-with-a-red-process-test-is-no-green-end 35628, seon.agent-test/a-death-costs-the-test-it-interrupted-and-no-other 34017. Each booted root costs its own process group (seon root, bin/held, embed-worker, fork server): one gate ran ~74 at once, load 70 (runs/evidence/load70-1026). Change: the harness (in-agent, start-root!) asks the running runtime for a fork on the test's branch env, so a test's system is a fork in milliseconds; a test whose subject is a boot itself (an update, a restart) keeps one boot, named. Deletes: start-root!'s bin/system start for every test but the boot tests, and each test's own root's store clone. Estimate: a fork ~361 ms against a boot's ~20 s (runtime's measure); the 1,541 s fall toward the tests' own work. The design (owner, 2026-11-14: \"our test fixtures should handle all thought so agents can't write shitty tests\"): an agent writes only the assertion; the harness supplies everything else. (1) The fork: every process, agent or test child, is handed its {:branch :commit :t}, and its first act is seon.program/hold-env! (runtime's, plans/runtime-tests-fork-on-env.md), which brings program and config to it whatever its parent held; only what cannot change in place (a pin, a native library, the binary) routes the work to a process on the right binary. One entry, routing by data: the harness forks from whatever runs and relies on that. (2) Fixtures forced before timing (seon.proofs/warm-fixtures!), never paid inside a budget (measured today: the world at 3.2M, a test world's graph at 40M); the first named case, web's, done (tests-4, 2026-11-14): seon.check-test/the-health-step-reads-a-base-whose-alias-the-head-retargets imported the program inside its budget (945M of a 1B red was seon.source/text-tx); it now reads the checkout's tree from the shard's program fixture (test-runner/kept-program), 6.4M against 9M estimated. (3) The budget from the declared estimate: a cost proof passes within 2x of its declared estimate, and past 2x is a finding whose cause is fixed, never a raised number (owner, 2026-11-14: \"do not be pedantic anything close is fine\", after a cost proof failed a land 0.08% over its 1.25x bound; and \"within 2x is met\"); when the harness lands, the existing cost bounds take the same rule. (4) Writes to the test's own branch only, never the standing main. (5) A mutation check that the assertion can go red. Booting a system, paying setup inside the budget, asserting on wall time or writing the standing main are impossible to express (a typed error), not laws caught after. Adopted additions: a green and a red at one digest with ticks over 10x apart files a :platform problem; low quality means an assertion no mutation reddens, and a deletion names it; the suite budget is the sum of declared estimates, ~300 s. Measured split: 201 of the 223 boot only through in-agent's shared root (seon.test-store/shared-root!, one boot a shard), 21 boot directly (start-root!, binary-sh): the harness change moves the 201, then the 21 slowest first. Named by context (2026-11-14) as waiting, not work (wall far above ticks): seon.turn-test/a-run-keeps-the-program-it-began-on (44.7 s wall against a 3M-tick estimate), seon.agent-test/a-broken-parser-reaches-the-model and seon.agent-test/an-agent-record-the-branch-holds-for-a-dead-process-becomes-this-processs; each is a check of the harness move. A test that still boots carries :seon.test/boots with its reason (only booting the system to its running state qualifies), and one that reaches start-root! without it is red. Target (the lower bound, AGENTS 3a.1): T(d) = plan(d) + R(d) x 21 ms / cores + 65 ms + B x 20 s (docs/cost.md: a warm-fork test 21 ms, a fork 65 ms; a boot ~20 s); a typical change (R ~216, today's small landings) ~1.3 s on 18 cores, a pin change (all 1,998 tests, B ~8 sharing one boot) ~25 s. The owner: within 2x is met (<= ~2.6 s, <= ~50 s); past 2x is a finding fixed at its cause, never a raised number; a cost proof states it. When the harness lands, :seon.config.proofs/gate-ms (today 10x a ~250 s gate) comes down with the gate's estimate to its lower bound (~25 s) in the same landing. The series (root, 2026-11-14: the top item, one series with the gate and verdicts as entities; nothing built on the old service): proving runs as Flow, in runtime's agreed shape (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer; its notes, runtime-effects-owner-shape.md): no new proc (runtime's correction, the owner's rule: no new process until the existing one is ruled out): the effects owner that exists, seon.fx/owner-proc (one per kind in system.edn's :fx map), gains a :process kind; its :in/:ask is the request port and :seon.config.fx/buf the back-pressure, :done carries the streamed results, :due is new; admission moves into owner-proc as a pure fn of (config, state), from max-cores x cores, free memory and peak weight, replacing every kind's :workers count, and the landing deletes the :item and :tests kinds, seon.prove's grant and admission and held-processes' ps read, a root-booting run weighing the whole admission until its peak is read; a request {:seon.step/request-id :seon.process/env {:branch :commit :t} :seon.fx/run {:seon.fx.run/tests [..] | :seon.fx.run/check step} :seon.bound/bound} goes to the agent holding that env (it forks the test child; another env spawns an agent on that branch), each test's result streams up and one :seon.fx/answer ends it; :ms is enforced by one put-after! and a group kill, answered :seon.bound/reached, never a cached red. Runtime builds the proc, ports, admission, deadline and env route, and deletes seon.prove's grant and admission (the interim never-read-class rule with them). Tests builds: (1) the :seon.fx/run payload and the child's per-test result, each a :seon.proof entity (data's schema) written by the owner through the one write path onto a results branch, cached only from a child whose hold-env! held the request's env, carrying the processes its run held at peak (admission's weight, no ps reading); (2) the merge check as the same request on the candidate branch's env (the gate's worktree and rebuild deleted: seon.reconcile/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch); (3) run-children!'s respawn of a dead child's shard deleted: a dead child is :seon.process/end on the answer; (4) a stop or a restart answers every queued and in-flight request with the typed :seon.system/ended (runtime widens it: :agent optional, cause :restart or :stop; no new key), never silence and never a re-ask by the caller (it completes web's seon.prove/a-prove-cut-by-a-restart-is-asked-again: a cut prove's greens are proof-facts already, so asked again it reuses them; web's relay passes it through as is, runtime's stop sends it); (5) check steps become tests; (6) the in-agent harness forks on the branch env (hold-env! slice 1 is on main, so this starts without runtime), then the 21 direct boots slowest first, low-quality ones deleted by name. Agreed with data (2026-11-14): the writer writes one :seon.proof/proof-fact per [test reads-digest] through the one write path, :seon.proof/reads beside :seon.proof/read-keys, its measures as :seon.proof/cost {:seon.bound/ticks :seon.bound/bytes :seon.bound/ms} (the bound's shape; it replaces :seon.bound/spent on proof-fact, removed in the same landing; the answer message keeps :seon.bound/spent), :seon.proof/peak-processes (nat-int); both optional in proof-fact, entries landed with the writer, data reviews; one results branch per store, :seon.config.proofs/results-branch (tests' config entry); :seon.proof/status is write-once, so a second different status is refused, and that refusal is the derived flaky problem (with seon.prove/ticks-apart's evidence), never an overwrite; reuse by data's seon.db/results-moved. Current verdict (data, 2026-11-14): each test's var entity on a branch holds :seon.test/verdict, a ref to its current proof-fact, set by the writer and retracted when results-moved finds the test's read-keys among moved datoms (no current verdict until a rerun); this replaces the :seon.test/status, digest and failures facts. Not green now is an AVET seek of :seon.proof/status in #{:fail :error} kept to proofs some :seon.test/verdict points to, O(reds), never history; :seon.test/proved becomes a pull of the verdict's status. The proof id's digest takes :seon.proof/program, and its description says so. The writer's landing carries :seon.test/verdict, the :seon.proof/status and reads-digest indexes and that description, data reviewing. The streamed message is {:seon.step/request-id n :seon.fx.run/result <proof-fact>} (runtime). THE FIRST SLICE (root, 2026-11-14 15:30, after load 64 from five worktree proves each sizing its own shards; Stage 1's first safety item): (a) admission in seon.fx/owner-proc as a pure fn of (config, state), from max-cores x cores and each class's peak weight (an unread class runs alone), replacing every kind's :workers; (b) bin/prove becomes a client that sends one :process request to the standing owner, its shards' sizing (:seon.config.proofs/jobs, worth-shards) deleted. Condition: before any session's prove routes to the standing owner, a proof shows N proves at once (n <= 3, real proves, no made-up load) keep the load within the cores. The result writer stays held for root's reads survey. Design and deletes: root's notes tests-process-kind-design.md, to be folded here when built. First proof, red first: a request on a branch's env answered with its verdicts, each a :seon.proof entity, and no respawn when a child dies. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.test-store/a-run-boots-one-root-per-test-group (Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\"; \"how the fuck do we have a load over 40\"); seon.test-store/a-process-test-costs-its-boots-not-its-work (Measured 2026-11-06 (docs/testing.md#a-root-costs-what-it-boots): bin/test --all was 1,931 s of tests serial, 9.5 min on 4 shards, 42 tests of 15 s or more being 73 percent of it; they did 2-9 M ticks and waited on root boots)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/agent-tests-sets-differ-only-tuned-keys-share seon.test-store-test/a-process-tests-system-is-a-fork-of-the-running-runtime}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-kept-program-store-is-one-store-a-tree-added-never-replaced"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-14 [M, tests-4]: the kept program store (seon.test-store/kept-program-store, one LMDB store per program-schema key holding a branch per git tree, each branched from its nearest kept tree and refreshed) is moved aside whole (test-runner/move-aside!) by any plan that must import a tree into a store past :seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes (256 MB). Every concurrent run whose plan kept its tree there then imports the program again inside a test (seon.check-test/the-health-step-reads-a-base-whose-alias-the-head-retargets: 1B ticks, red, data's land, reproduced). Tonight program-store-25acba99 was moved at 21:20 and held 9 trees and 187 MB of data.mdb (318 MB with its per-tree reach-graph and reads-index fasl) by 21:31: the bound is reached about every 15 min with 4 lands at once. Tried first [M, tests-4's helper, 2026-11-14]: one store per tree made by copying the nearest tree's store fails: a copied LMDB store keeps its source's id (seon.db/store-cfg) and Datahike refuses a second connection of one id in a process (:config-does-not-match-existing-connections), which a shard holding its tree while health makes HEAD's always does; the attempt is /tmp/tests4/helper-kept-store-per-tree.patch, not landed. Correct: generations of the key's store, added and never replaced. A full store (past :seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes) is never moved: the next tree to import starts the key's next generation, <base>-<key>-<n>, a whole import made by the plan (outside every test's budget); a tree is read from whichever generation holds its branch, newest first; a generation past :seon.config.proofs/program-stores-kept that no process holds open (seon.test-store/held-open?) is removed. Estimate: a store fills at 256 MB, ~9 trees tonight (187 MB data.mdb at 9 [M]); so one whole import (~1B ticks, ~30-60 s, the plan's) per ~9 new trees, against today's re-import by every run that read the moved store (one inside a test: 1B ticks, red); a tree found costs one branch? a generation, k generations kept (k = 3: 3 seeks); disk k x 256 MB as today's bound. Deletes: move-aside! and the .retired-* sweep; the bound stays, read only to start a generation. Proof, red first: two runs on two trees at once, the second's store made while the first's shard reads its own, and the first reads its tree to the end with no import (seon.proofs-test/a-new-trees-store-never-replaces-a-store-a-run-reads)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-new-trees-store-never-replaces-a-store-a-run-reads}}
